              "question": "<p>Most benzodiazepines end in the suffix …zepam or …zolam. Which of the following is NOT a benzodiazepine?</p>\n"
            },
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "metadata": {
              "contentType": "Multiple Choice",
              "license": "U",
              "title": "Untitled Multiple Choice"
            },
            "subContentId": "c6f9b970-66a3-4b4c-ae98-d0046967786e"
          },
          {
            "params": {
              "media": {
                "disableImageZooming": false
              },
              "answers": [
                {
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  },
                  "text": "Benzodiazepines potentiate the action of the inhibitory neurotransmitter GABA at the interneurons in the brain, meaning that they increase the amount of inhibition in the brain.&nbsp;\n"
                },
                {
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  },
                  "text": "Generally, the benzodiazepines that come on quickly and have a short half life are better as hypnotics (sleep inducing medications) while those that are long lasting are better for muscle spasm\n"
                },
                {
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  },
                  "text": "Diazepam, first sold as Valium has a very long half life (50 hours) and is commonly used to treat anxiety, panic attacks and muscle spasms\n"
                },
                {
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  },
                  "text": "Long term administration of benzodiazepines increases the risk of developing Alzheimer’s disease and there is a potential for addiction and abuse\n"
                },
                {
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Your answer was incorrect. The higher the dose, and the longer duration of treatment with benzodiazepines, the greater risk of developing Alzheimer's disease.&nbsp;So a client should be placed on the smallest effective dose for the shortest time.&nbsp;</div>\n",
                    "notChosenFeedback": "<div>Well done, the higher the dose, and the longer the duration of treatment with benzodiazepines, the greater risk of developing Alzheimer's disease. So a client should be placed on the smallest effective dose for the shortest time.&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>\n",
                    "tip": ""
                  },
                  "text": "<div>While treatment with benzodiazepines is associated with the development of Alzheimer disease, dose or duration of use are not factors in this correlation.&nbsp;</div>\n"
                }
              ],
